\details{
\strong{Method}

UMAP (McInnes et al. 2018) builds a weighted graph of each sample's
nearest neighbours, then finds plot coordinates whose own neighbour graph
is as similar to it as possible.
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Neighbours.} Each sample's \code{n_neighbors} nearest neighbours,
counting itself, are found exactly from \code{dm}. Ties go to the sample
that comes first.
\item \strong{Graph.} A neighbour \eqn{j} of sample \eqn{i} at distance
\eqn{d_{ij}} gets weight \eqn{\exp(-(d_{ij} - \rho_i) / \sigma_i)},
where \eqn{\rho_i} is the distance to the nearest neighbour (other than
duplicates) and \eqn{\sigma_i} is chosen by binary search so that the
weights sum to \eqn{\log_2(}\code{n_neighbors}\eqn{)}. The two weights
\eqn{w_{ij}} and \eqn{w_{ji}} of each pair are combined by fuzzy union,
\eqn{w_{ij} + w_{ji} - w_{ij} w_{ji}}.
\item \strong{Curve.} \code{a} and \code{b} are fitted by least squares (Levenberg-Marquardt,
as umap-learn fits them) to a curve that is 1 up to \code{min_dist} and decays
exponentially, with scale \code{spread}, beyond it.
\item \strong{Layout.} Starting from \code{init}, stochastic gradient descent pulls
the two samples of each edge together, sampling each edge in proportion
to its weight, and pushes each sample away from \code{negative_sample_rate}
randomly chosen samples per edge sampled. As in \code{uwot::umap()}'s batch
mode, each epoch computes every sample's gradient from the coordinates
at the start of the epoch, then moves all samples at once by the Adam
optimizer (\eqn{\beta_1 = 0.5}, \eqn{\beta_2 = 0.9},
\eqn{\epsilon = 10^{-7}}, uwot's settings for batch mode). Finally, the
coordinates are centered.
}

\strong{Why batch mode}

\code{uwot::umap()}'s default optimization moves samples one edge at a time,
each move seeing the ones before it. That can only run on one thread if
it is to be reproducible, and it takes nearly all of the run time. In
batch mode, the samples' gradients within an epoch don't depend on each
other, so \code{umap()} computes them on \code{cpus} threads, and still gets the
same result for any number of threads. The layouts differ from those of
uwot's default mode only as different random draws would: neighbour
preservation, trustworthiness, and the final cross-entropy are about the
same.

\strong{Starting coordinates}
\itemize{
\item \code{"spectral"}: The eigenvectors of the graph's normalized Laplacian for
its 2nd to \eqn{(k+1)}th smallest eigenvalues, scaled so that the
largest absolute coordinate is 10, plus normal noise with standard
deviation 0.0001. When the graph is not connected, the \code{"pcoa"} start is
used instead, scaled to standard deviation 1, and with fewer than 3
samples (or \eqn{k > n - 2}), the \code{"random"} one, as in \code{uwot::umap()}.
\item \code{"pcoa"}: The first \code{n_axes} axes of \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, each centered and scaled to
standard deviation 0.0001 (\code{uwot::umap()}'s \code{"spca"}).
\item \code{"random"}: Coordinates uniform on [-10, 10).
\item A matrix: Used as is.
}

\strong{Differences from \code{uwot::umap()}}
\itemize{
\item \code{umap()} always optimizes in batch mode, so its layouts are those of
\code{uwot::umap(batch = TRUE)}, not of uwot's default.
\item \code{min_dist} defaults to \code{0.1}, the canonical value used by umap-learn and
most other implementations, rather than uwot's \code{0.01}. Other defaults
are the same.
\item The random numbers come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number
generator, so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and R's random number stream
is left untouched. In batch mode, \code{uwot::umap()} draws from R's
generator two 32-bit seeds per sample per epoch, for each sample's
stream of negative samples, and the jitter of spectral starts. \code{umap()}
makes those from \code{seed} instead, with the same streams of negative
samples (PCG) from the same seeds; given the same seeds and starting
coordinates, the two give the same coordinates (to the last bit, for
the same compiler and math library).
\item The spectral start's eigenvectors are computed by block Lanczos
iteration, the same solver as \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s, to relative residuals of
\eqn{10^{-14}} (at worst \eqn{10^{-10}}) rather than \code{RSpectra}'s
tolerance of \eqn{10^{-4}}, and each one's sign is fixed (its largest
absolute coordinate is positive).
\item The \code{"pcoa"} start, and the fallback for unconnected graphs, come from
\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}} rather than \code{cmdscale()}; they differ only by rounding and the
signs of the axes.
\item Finally, each axis is flipped so that its largest absolute coordinate
is positive.
}

\strong{Speed and memory}

\code{uwot::umap()} given a \code{dist} object copies it into an \eqn{n \times n}
matrix and sorts each of its columns in R. \code{umap()} reads the packed
triangle of \code{dm} directly, keeping only the \code{n_neighbors} closest of each
sample. Beyond \code{dm} itself, memory use is proportional to \eqn{n} times
\code{n_neighbors}. \code{cpus} threads find the neighbours, build the graph,
compute the spectral start, and compute each epoch's gradients.

\strong{Reproducibility}

Results are identical from run to run and for any number of \code{cpus}.
}
\section{Ordination result}{

Every ordination function (\code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}, \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}}, \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}}, \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}}, \code{\link[=capda]{capda()}},
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}}, \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, \code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, and so
\code{\link[=dist_ord]{dist_ord()}}) returns the same kind of
object: a numeric matrix with one row per sample and one column per
axis, so that it can go straight into a data frame. Rows are
named after the samples, when the input has names. Columns are named
after the method: \code{"PCoA1"}, \code{"PC1"}, \code{"RPCA1"}, \code{"dbRDA1"} or \code{"CAP1"}
(then \code{"MDS1"} for unconstrained axes), \code{"MDS1"}, \code{"NMDS1"},
\code{"Isomap1"}, \code{"TSNE1"}, \code{"UMAP1"}, and so on.

Its attributes always begin with these three, in this order, each with
one value per column:
\itemize{
\item \code{method}: The method's ID, e.g. \code{"pcoa"} or \code{"nmds"}.
\item \code{eig}: Each axis's eigenvalue, on the method's own scale: \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}
and \code{\link[=isomap]{isomap()}} give the eigenvalues of Gower's centered matrix (sums
of squares); \code{\link[=pca]{pca()}} and \code{\link[=rpca]{rpca()}} give variances, which are \code{\link[=pcoa]{pcoa()}}'s
divided by \eqn{n - 1} for \eqn{n} samples; and \code{\link[=dbrda]{dbrda()}} and
\code{\link[=capscale]{capscale()}} follow \code{vegan}, which also divides by \eqn{n - 1} when any
distance is 4 or more. \code{NA} for \code{\link[=mds]{mds()}}, \code{\link[=nmds]{nmds()}}, \code{\link[=tsne]{tsne()}}, and
\code{\link[=umap]{umap()}}, which have no eigenvalues.
\item \code{prop}: The proportion of the total variance (or inertia) that each
axis explains. Unlike \code{eig}, it is comparable between methods, so use
it to label axes. \code{NA} where it isn't defined.
}

Attributes particular to the method follow them, and last \code{display},
with the ordination's title for \code{plot()} and \code{print()}: the method's
name, and for the methods that take distances, the distance matrix's
title, such as "Principal Coordinates Analysis of Bray-Curtis
Dissimilarity" (see \link{ecodive_display}). The matrix has class
\code{"ecodive_ord"} (as well as \code{"matrix"}), so that \code{print()} lists the
other attributes by name rather than in full, and \code{plot()} draws the
samples on the first two axes, labelled from the attributes, with any
loadings, constraints and centroids on top (see \code{\link[=plot.ecodive_ord]{plot.ecodive_ord()}}).
\code{plot(ord, "Species", iris)} colours the samples by a column of
metadata. Subsetting with \code{[}, or \code{as.data.frame()}, gives a plain
matrix or data frame without the attributes.
}

